  "step_3": "The decision reason is about preferring a more left-skewed lottery — meaning a greater probability of favorable outcomes (positive) relative to unfavorable ones. However, in both lotteries, the outcomes are negative (losses), and A has a 20% chance of zero loss (favorable), while B has 100% loss (unfavorable). The distribution of A is heavily weighted toward loss (80%), so it is actually right-skewed (mass on the left, negative side), not left-skewed. B is degenerate and has no skewness. Thus, neither lottery is left-skewed, so the decision reason does not apply. The individual’s reasoning focuses on the possibility of avoiding a loss in A, which is a different criterion (risk avoidance or hope for zero loss) than skewness. There is no mention or application of skewness in the verbal report. Therefore, the individual did not use the decision reason. The reason is not triggered by the data, and the justification provided does not reflect the rule of skewness preference.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
